.TH EUCLID-CLI-EKV-GET-ITEM 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.73" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ekv-get-item \- read one item by its key
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli ekv get-item
.BR \-\-table | \-t
.I TABLE
.RB { \-\-key | \-k
.I JSON
|
.B \-\-key\-file
.IR PATH }
.SH DESCRIPTION
Reads one item and prints it as it was written, plus
.I _created
and
.IR _modified .
.PP
The key is an object of the table's key attributes and nothing else \-
.I {"supplierId":"4711"}
for a table keyed on
.I supplierId
alone, and both attributes for a table with a sort key. Extra
attributes are refused rather than ignored, because a key that is not
exactly the key is usually a caller who thinks the table is keyed on
something else.
.PP
An item that is not there is an error, not an empty answer: "there is
no such item" and "here is an item with nothing in it" are different
things, and a caller should not have to tell them apart by inspecting
what came back.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-table " " \fITABLE\fR
Table to read from. Required.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key " " \fIJSON\fR
The key attributes, as a JSON object.
.TP
.BR \-\-key\-file " " \fIPATH\fR
A file holding the key as JSON. Give one of this and
.BR \-\-key ,
not both.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The item is printed.
.TP
.B 1
No such table, no such item, the key was missing an attribute or
carried one of the wrong type, or it named an attribute the table is
not keyed on.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli ekv get-item --table suppliers --key '{"supplierId":"4711"}'

euclid-cli ekv get-item --table deliveries \e
    --key '{"supplierId":"4711","deliveredAt":"2026-09-10T22:00:00Z"}'

# One field of it
euclid-cli ekv get-item -t suppliers -k '{"supplierId":"4711"}' \e
    | jq -r .delivery.format
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-ekv (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekv-put-item (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekv-query (1)
